@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+# Experimental KVM GICv2 support
+
+This branch adds an experimental AArch64 GICv2 backend for hosts such as Raspberry Pi 5. Upstream v53.0 requires GICv3. The port is being validated separately from the Kuberoot release image; a successful emulator test does not establish physical board support.
+
+KVM's device capability test selects GICv3 when available. It falls back to GICv2 only when the GICv3 probe returns `ENODEV`; permission failures and other errors are returned to the caller. The capability test does not create or take ownership of a device file descriptor.
+
+## Device model
+
+The GICv2 configuration uses a banked CPU interface and supports one to eight vCPUs. By default, `boot_vcpus` must equal `max_vcpus`. The experimental CPU hot-add opt-in described below reserves a fixed controller capacity while publishing a growing CPU prefix. The distributor exposes 256 interrupt IDs. Legacy device interrupts retain their existing allocation; a separate range of 64 SPIs is assigned to the MSI frame.
+
+| Component | Guest physical address | Size |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| GICv2 distributor | `0x08fff000` | 4 KiB |
+| GICv2 CPU interface | `0x08ffd000` | 8 KiB |
+| GICv2m MSI frame | `0x08ffc000` | 4 KiB |
+| MSI interrupt range | INTID 128–191 | 64 SPIs |
+
+These addresses belong to the alternative GICv2 layout. The existing GICv3 distributor, redistributor and ITS layout remains unchanged.
+
+The FDT describes `arm,cortex-a15-gic`, its actual distributor and CPU-interface ranges, and an `arm,gic-v2m-frame` child when MSI is configured. MADT uses GIC version 2, per-CPU GICC interface addresses and an MSI frame entry. GICv2 does not advertise redistributors, an ITS or an IORT ITS group. The existing GICv3 and MSHV table paths are preserved.
+
+## Interrupt delivery
+
+The MSI frame implements the non-secure register interface in [Arm BSA DEN0094A, Appendix I](https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5fae475aca04df4095c1c97a). `MSI_TYPER` describes the immutable SPI range; `MSI_IIDR` is zero and does not select a vendor-specific MSI-data quirk. `MSI_SETSPI_NS` accepts little-endian 16-bit low-half writes and 32-bit writes. Bits `[9:0]` select the absolute SPI; writes outside the advertised range do not inject an interrupt. Reserved register reads return zero and reserved writes have no effect.
+
+A direct guest MMIO write triggers an IRQ group indexed by absolute SPI. PCI MSI/MSI-X irqfds use the same SPI range. Since ARM KVM's MSI route requires an ITS, the KVM backend validates the MSI address and SPI and translates a GICv2m message into an IRQCHIP route. Its pin is `SPI - 32`, as required by ARM KVM; MSI-only flags and device IDs are cleared. Cached device routing entries retain their MSI representation, and GICv3 routing is unchanged. The guest's MSI driver programs the GIC SPI as edge-triggered.
+
+The frame has no writable configuration or separate pending-interrupt state. Pending interrupts belong to the GIC and IRQ routing. This does not make the complete VM migratable.
+
+## Validation boundaries
+
+The development checks are split into independently recorded steps:
+
+1. A KVM ioctl probe inside a GICv2/EL2 emulator verifies vCPU creation, VGICv2 initialization and register access, and the expected VGICv3 `ENODEV` result.
+2. A direct-kernel guest checks the GICv2 description, timer and PL011 interrupt counters and clean shutdown. PCI is disabled for this diagnostic step.
+3. PCI validation must enable the MSI frame and exercise actual virtio disk and network traffic, with interrupt counters and data-integrity checks. A kernel reaching userspace with PCI disabled is insufficient.
+4. The target firmware path must boot the pinned Cloud Hypervisor EDK2 firmware and a persistent guest disk, using the generated ACPI tables. Firmware boot, guest reboot and I/O require separate evidence.
+5. Physical Raspberry Pi 5 validation must verify the real boot path, EL2/KVM availability, native guest execution, NVMe and networking. Emulator results do not substitute for this check.
+
+## Experimental GICv2 state format
+
+`GicState::KvmV2` contains a separately versioned format (`format_version: 1`). The existing `Kvm` GICv3 JSON representation and its legacy untagged decoder are unchanged. The new format records the exact fixed CPU IDs, interrupt count, MMIO layout, distributor and CPU-interface identifiers, shared SPI banks, banked SGI/PPI state, CPU interface registers and all SGI source masks. KVM's PMR value is stored in its raw five-bit ABI representation.
+
+Capture requires all vCPUs and interrupt producers to be quiesced. Restore requires a fresh initialized target before its first `KVM_RUN`, with matching CPU register state and guest clock restored before resume. All serialized lengths, register masks, topology and controller identifiers are checked before the first write. IIDR is written first to enable interrupt-group restoration. Enable, pending and active banks use clear-and-set operations. SGI source masks are restored through `CPENDSGIR`/`SPENDSGIR`; setting generic SGI pending bits would invent the destination CPU as a source. An ioctl error during restore leaves the target unusable until it is discarded; a failed restore is not permission to run it.
+
+Format 1 has deliberate limits:
+
+- Active SGIs are rejected. The KVM v2 ABI does not export their active source ID, so restoring only the active bit could change subsequent SGI delivery.
+- Pending edge-triggered SPIs and pending SGIs with consistent source masks are supported. Pending level-triggered SPIs and PPIs other than architectural timer PPIs 27 and 30 are rejected because their input-line restoration is not established for this device model. This is a current implementation boundary, not a claim that level interrupts cannot be migrated.
+- Timer PPI pending state is accepted together with the CPU/clock restore contract. KVM recomputes the architectural timer level from timer control and compare registers on vCPU load. An active level interrupt whose pending bit is clear can be restored with its active state preserved.
+- The current target has five priority bits. Nonzero additional APR banks are rejected; distributor IIDR revisions other than 2 or 3 are rejected. A change in controller capacity or topology is unsupported.
+
+The register ABI is documented in [Linux KVM VGICv2](https://docs.kernel.org/virt/kvm/devices/arm-vgic.html). The implementation assumptions were checked against Linux 6.18.55 [`vgic-mmio-v2.c`](https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/v6.18.55/arch/arm64/kvm/vgic/vgic-mmio-v2.c), [`vgic-mmio.c`](https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/v6.18.55/arch/arm64/kvm/vgic/vgic-mmio.c) and [`arch_timer.c`](https://github.com/gregkh/linux/blob/v6.18.55/arch/arm64/kvm/arch_timer.c).
+
+The pure register-model tests exercise exact restoration over dirty target state, foreign SGI sources, invalid input before mutation, active-SGI and unknown-level-source rejection, and GICv3 serialization compatibility. `test_v2_kvm_ioctl_state_roundtrip_and_rejections` requires an ARM KVM host with VGICv2 support and creates two independent VMs without executing either. It tests actual register transfer, timer/SGI pending state and rejection paths. It is a separate hardware test, not a substitute for a paused guest snapshot/restore with timer, disk and network continuity.
+
+Live migration remains explicitly disabled for GICv2. Restoring interrupt-controller registers does not solve receive-side memory admission, asynchronous migration outcomes or source/target ownership after a lost completion acknowledgement. Full guest lifecycle and physical board validation remain required before this backend is declared ready for the product.
+
+## Experimental CPU hot-add
+
+`--cpus boot=1,max=4,acpi_hotplug=on` (JSON `cpus.acpi_hotplug: true`) opts in to the bounded ARM KVM/GICv2 path. It requires `--firmware` with the supported Cloud Hypervisor EDK2 UEFI and an ACPI guest; `1 <= boot <= max <= 8`. A guest booted with ACPI disabled is outside this contract. The default is off, and existing x86 and MSHV paths are unchanged. The explicit `--firmware` runtime path must be validated separately from the legacy `--kernel` firmware fallback.
+
+All `max` KVM vCPU objects are created before GIC initialization, then configured once. MADT advertises all of their MPIDRs with GICC `ONLINE_CAPABLE`; only boot CPUs have `ENABLED`. Dormant CPU ACPI devices return `_STA = 0xd`, becoming `0xf` when published. The guest receives a DeviceCheck through the existing GED/CPU controller. CPU removal is not advertised, and raw eject writes are ignored in this mode. Resize currently accepts a running VM and growth only; reducing the count requires restarting the guest.
+
+New threads complete affinity, core-scheduling, signal and seccomp setup behind an abortable gate. No new CPU is published until the whole batch is ready. Setup/spawn failure aborts and joins the new batch while existing CPUs continue. After publication, a notification failure retains the actual count and pending insertion state; retrying the same requested count reissues the notification. VMM availability does not establish guest CPU onlining, which requires guest policy and separate runtime verification.
+
+The CPU-manager snapshot has a versioned active-prefix record separate from allocated capacity, including pending notifications. Restore checks metadata and all CPU snapshots before creating vCPUs, restores all reserved CPU states, and starts only the saved active prefix. Restored active threads must park before guest-clock restoration and resume. A target that fails this handshake must be discarded. Legacy snapshots and GICv3 state encoding are unchanged.
+
+Pure tests cover the gate's failure/timeout/publication ordering, snapshot metadata rejection, dormant MADT flags, ARM AML status and raw eject rejection. ARM hot-add acceptance still requires UEFI cold boot, growth without a boot-ID reset, guest onlining, continued timer/disk/network activity, and snapshots with dormant reserved CPUs. No live-migration guard is removed by this work.
